{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"bva4b6eha","tool_use_id":"toolu_01SFkhW5KtyTutF9eD1e4LDZ","status":"completed","output_file":"/tmp/claude-441489/-scratch-users-kushinm-auto-psych-outer-loop-live-runs-run3-repo/edbc4288-57ff-4d36-bca0-6656a5f6685f/tasks/bva4b6eha.output","summary":"Background command \"cd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop; D=iter_4/candidate_4\ncat << 'EOF' > $D/hypothesis.md\nRefinement of the incumbent `second_order_motif_side_habit`: people still judge a sequence random to the extent their own second-order model of a fair coin (a person-specific believed switch rate, and a shared shift in how likely a switch is right after a switch) explains it better than the regular generators (a switch-biased Markov coin, a trick coin, and a repeating short motif with slips, with each person's own suspicion of motifs), the evidence weighed per flip by a fitted power of length, with each person's own side habit deciding near-ties. The single change, taken from `heads_rigged_trick_coin_suspicion`, is that the trick coin people suspect is not symmetric: they believe rigged coins are more often rigged towards heads (fitted direction and strength), so a surplus of heads is readily explained away as rigging while an equal surplus of tails is not, and tail-heavy sequences look more random than their head-heavy mirror images — addressing the FDR-surviving critique that people choose the head-heavier sequence less often than the H/T-symmetric incumbent predicts. Unlike a tail-favouring belief about the fair coin itself (linear in the head count), this asymmetry acts only through the rigging explanation, so it matters most for clearly lopsided sequences and fades for near-balanced ones.\nEOF\necho second_order_heads_rigged_side_habit > $D/model_name.txt\npython3 - << 'PY'\nsrc = open(\"models/second_order_motif_side_habit.py\").read()\ndoc_end = src.index('\"\"\"', 3) + 3\nnewdoc = '''\"\"\"Heads-rigged trick coin added to the second-order motif-suspicion judgment with side habits.\n\nRefinement of `second_order_motif_side_habit` (described below). Single change,\ntaken from `heads_rigged_trick_coin_suspicion`: the biased-coin generator's\nheads rate has a Beta(2*sigmoid(s), 2*(1 - sigmoid(s))) prior with a fitted\nasymmetry s (s = 0 recovers the uniform, symmetric prior), so with s > 0 a surplus\nof heads is evidence of rigging while an equal surplus of tails is not, and\ntail-heavy sequences look more random than their head-heavy mirror images.\nAddresses the FDR-surviving critique that people choose the head-heavier sequence\nless often than the H/T-symmetric incumbent predicts.\n\nDescription of the incumbent:\n''' + src[3:doc_end]\nsrc = newdoc + src[doc_end:]\nsrc = src.replace('''    out = {\"k\": float(k), \"lm\": log_markov, \"lb\": log_biased, \"alt2\": float(ss - sr)}''',\n '''    out = {\"k\": float(k), \"lm\": log_markov, \"lb\": log_biased, \"alt2\": float(ss - sr), \"h\": float(h)}''')\nsrc = src.replace('''        \"seq_len\": np.asarray([t[\"n\"] for t in table], dtype=\"float64\"),\n    }''', '''        \"seq_len\": np.asarray([t[\"n\"] for t in table], dtype=\"float64\"),\n        \"seq_heads\": np.asarray([t[\"h\"] for t in table], dtype=\"float64\"),\n    }''')\nsrc = src.replace('''    seq_len = pm.Data(\"seq_len\", np.full(1, 2.0))\n''', '''    seq_len = pm.Data(\"seq_len\", np.full(1, 2.0))\n    seq_heads = pm.Data(\"seq_heads\", np.zeros(1, dtype=\"float64\"))\n''')\nsrc = src.replace('''    # Log marginal likelihood of each distinct sequence under the regular generators.''',\n'''    # Asymmetric trick coin: heads-rate prior Beta(a_h, a_t), a_h + a_t = 2\n    # (s = 0 recovers the uniform, symmetric prior).\n    s_asym = pm.Normal(\"s_asym\", mu=0.0, sigma=1.0)\n    a_h = 2.0 * pm.math.sigmoid(s_asym)\n    a_t = 2.0 - a_h\n    seq_lbias_asym = (\n        pt.gammaln(seq_heads + a_h)\n        + pt.gammaln(seq_len - seq_heads + a_t)\n        - pt.gammaln(seq_len + 2.0)\n        - pt.gammaln(a_h)\n        - pt.gammaln(a_t)\n    )\n\n    # Log marginal likelihood of each distinct sequence under the regular generators.''')\nsrc = src.replace(\"seq_lbias[idx]\", \"seq_lbias_asym[idx]\")\nopen(\"iter_4/candidate_4/candidate.py\", \"w\").write(src)\nPY\ngrep -n \"seq_lbias\\|seq_heads\\|s_asym\" $D/candidate.py\ncd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/runs/run3/repo && /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -m src.pipelines.inner_loop.check_candidate --candidate-dir /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_4 --responses /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/responses.csv 2>&1 | tail -5\" completed (exit code 0)","uuid":"702e4cbc-d2f4-4274-bcfc-083ffc0a0098","session_id":"edbc4288-57ff-4d36-bca0-6656a5f6685f"}
