{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfjDGepgiMkd6A1r9ef59","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate and it passes the admission self-check: it prints `OK`, and its ELPD-LOO on the short smoke fit is -2094.7. That's only a 100-draw, single-chain fit, so the number can't be compared directly with the incumbent's -2103.4. The self-check doesn't test novelty against the live models, convergence or the 30-minute speed limit; admission's full fit will decide those.\n\n**Model:** `second_order_heads_rigged_side_habit`, a refinement of the incumbent `second_order_motif_side_habit`.\n\n**The one change:** I took the lopsided trick-coin prior from `heads_rigged_trick_coin_suspicion`. Every other part of the incumbent stays as it was:\n- the second-order fair coin;\n- each person's own believed switch rate;\n- each person's own suspicion of repeating motifs;\n- weighing the evidence per flip by a fitted power of length;\n- each person's own side habit.\n\n**What it claims:** people believe a rigged coin is more often rigged towards heads, with the direction and strength fitted. So a sequence with extra heads is easily explained away as a rigged coin, while one with the same number of extra tails is not. Tail-heavy sequences therefore look more random than their head-heavy mirror images. This targets the one critique that survives the FDR correction: people pick the sequence with more heads less often than the incumbent predicts (0.473 observed against 0.488 expected, q = 0.032).\n\n**Why this rather than the face-bias graft:** the obvious fix is to graft the \"fair coin favours tails\" belief, but that is close to the live `face_bias_motif_side_habit` and `face_biased_second_order_motif` models. I chose this version to stay away from them. The head-count effect here works only through the rigged-coin explanation, so it is strongest for clearly lopsided sequences and fades for near-balanced ones. The face-bias belief instead shifts every sequence in proportion to its head count. The two should predict differently enough to pass the novelty gate, but the self-check didn't test that.\n\nAll three files are in `iter_4/candidate_4/`: `hypothesis.md`, `model_name.txt` and `candidate.py`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2715,"cache_read_input_tokens":91746,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2715,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":674,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"edbc4288-57ff-4d36-bca0-6656a5f6685f","uuid":"d96c213c-86e9-44a1-82a3-12f726152a5d","timestamp":"2026-10-05T14:01:25.688Z","request_id":"req_011CfjDGeNe4wJ7WamrwAanG"}
